For monoatomic gas A, degrees of freedom \(f_{A} = 3\) (3 translational).
For polyatomic gas B, degrees of freedom \(f_{B} = 3\) (translational) + \(3\) (rotational) + \(2 \times 1\) (vibrational mode) \(= 3+3+2 = 8\).
The specific heat ratio \(\gamma\) is given by \(\gamma = 1 + \frac{2}{f} = \frac{f+2}{f}\).
\frac{\gamma_{A}}{\gamma_{B}} = \frac{(f_{A}+2)/f_{A}}{(f_{B}+2)/f_{B}} = \frac{f_{A}+2}{f_{A}} \times \frac{f_{B}}{f_{B}+2}
= \frac{3+2}{3} \times \frac{8}{8+2}
= \frac{5}{3} \times \frac{8}{10} = \frac{40}{30}
Given that \(\frac{\gamma_{A}}{\gamma_{B}} = \left(1+\frac{1}{n}\right)\).
\therefore \frac{40}{30} = 1+\frac{1}{n}
\Rightarrow \frac{4}{3} = 1+\frac{1}{n}
\Rightarrow \frac{4}{3} - 1 = \frac{1}{n}
\Rightarrow \frac{1}{3} = \frac{1}{n}
\Rightarrow n = 3

### Step 1: Locate the image of point A

Using the lens formula \(\frac{1}{v} - \frac{1}{u} = \frac{1}{f}\).
\frac{1}{v} - \frac{1}{u} = \frac{1}{f}
Given object distance \(u = -30\text{ cm}\) (real object, left of lens) and focal length \(f = 20\text{ cm}\) (convex lens).
\frac{1}{v} - \frac{1}{-30} = \frac{1}{20}
\frac{1}{v} + \frac{1}{30} = \frac{1}{20}
\frac{1}{v} = \frac{1}{20} - \frac{1}{30} = \frac{3 - 2}{60} = \frac{1}{60}
v = 60\text{ cm}
The image A' of point A is formed at \(60\text{ cm}\) on the opposite side of the lens.

### Step 2: Calculate the transverse magnification (m)

Transverse magnification \(m = \frac{v}{u}\).
m = \frac{60}{-30} = -2
The negative sign indicates an inverted image.

### Step 3: Calculate the longitudinal magnification (m_L) and image dimensions

Longitudinal magnification \(m_L = \frac{dv}{du} = m^2\) for small objects.
The horizontal extent of the object is \(du = 1\text{ cm}\).
dv = m^2 du = (-2)^2 \times 1 = 4 \times 1 = 4\text{ cm}
The vertical height of the object is \(h_0 = 2\text{ cm}\).
h_i = m h_0 = -2 \times 2 = -4\text{ cm}
So, the image A'B' has a horizontal extent of \(4\text{ cm}\) and a vertical height of \(4\text{ cm}\) (inverted).

### Step 4: Determine the angle made by the image with the principal axis

The angle \(\theta\) made by the image with the principal axis can be found using the tangent of the angle.
\tan \theta_{\text{image}} = \frac{\text{vertical height of image}}{\text{horizontal extent of image}}
Considering the inverted nature of the image, the vertical height is \(-4\text{ cm}\) and the horizontal extent is \(4\text{ cm}\).
\tan \theta_{\text{image}} = \frac{-4\text{ cm}}{4\text{ cm}} = -1
\theta_{\text{image}} = \arctan(-1) = -45^{\circ}
The angle made by the image with the principal axis is \(-45^{\circ}\).

### Calculation of River Width


### Step 1: Determine the perpendicular component of boat velocity

The river flows from west to east. The boat moves at \(150^{\circ}\) to the direction of river flow. This means the angle of the boat's velocity with the direction perpendicular to the river flow (across the river) is \(150^{\circ} - 90^{\circ} = 60^{\circ}\) or \(90^{\circ} - (180^{\circ} - 150^{\circ}) = 90^{\circ} - 30^{\circ} = 60^{\circ}\). The component of the boat's velocity perpendicular to the river flow, \(V_{\perp}\), is responsible for crossing the river.
V_{\perp} = 27 \times \cos60^{\circ} = 27 \times \frac{1}{2} = \frac{27}{2} \text{ km/hr}

### Step 2: Convert units and calculate the width

Time taken to cross the river is half a minute, which is \(30\) seconds. To calculate the width in meters, we convert \(V_{\perp}\) from \(\text{km/hr}\) to \(\text{m/s}\).
V_{\perp} = \frac{27}{2} \times \frac{1000}{3600} \text{ m/s} = \frac{27}{2} \times \frac{5}{18} \text{ m/s}
The width of the river, \(S\), is given by the product of the perpendicular velocity component and the time taken.
S = V_{\perp} \times t = \left(\frac{27}{2} \times \frac{5}{18}\right) \times 30 \text{ m}
S = \frac{135}{36} \times 30 \text{ m} = \frac{15}{4} \times 30 \text{ m} = 15 \times 7.5 \text{ m} = 112.5 \text{ m}
The width of the river is \(112.5 \text{ m}\).

