### Understanding the problem and setting up equations

For a single slit diffraction pattern, the angular position of the \(n^{th}\) minimum is given by \(a \sin \theta = n \lambda\). Therefore, \(\theta = \sin^{-1}\left(\frac{n\lambda}{a}\right)\).
The second minimum to the left of the central maximum corresponds to \(n=2\). Let this angle be \(\theta_1\).
\theta_1 = \sin^{-1}\left(\frac{2\lambda}{a}\right)
The third minimum to the right of the central maximum corresponds to \(n=3\). Let this angle be \(\theta_2\).
\theta_2 = \sin^{-1}\left(\frac{3\lambda}{a}\right)
The measured angular separation between these two minima is \(30^\circ\).
\theta_1 + \theta_2 = 30^\circ = \frac{\pi}{6} \text{ radians}

### Exact Method

\sin^{-1}\left(\frac{2\lambda}{a}\right) + \sin^{-1}\left(\frac{3\lambda}{a}\right) = \frac{\pi}{6}
Using the identity \(\sin^{-1}x + \sin^{-1}y = \sin^{-1}(x\sqrt{1-y^2} + y\sqrt{1-x^2})\), we get:
\frac{2\lambda}{a}\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{3\lambda}{a}\right)^2} + \frac{3\lambda}{a}\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{2\lambda}{a}\right)^2} = \sin\frac{\pi}{6}
Given \(\lambda = 628\) nm. After solving this equation, the value of \(a\) is approximately:
a = 6.07 \mu m

### Approximate Method

For small angles, \(\sin \theta \approx \theta\) (in radians) and \(\sin^{-1}x \approx x\). Therefore, \(\theta_1 \approx \frac{2\lambda}{a}\) and \(\theta_2 \approx \frac{3\lambda}{a}\).
\theta_1 + \theta_2 = \frac{\pi}{6}
\frac{2\lambda}{a} + \frac{3\lambda}{a} = \frac{\pi}{6}
\frac{5\lambda}{a} = \frac{\pi}{6}
Substitute \(\lambda = 628\) nm \(= 628 \times 10^{-9}\) m.
a = \frac{5 \times 628 \text{ nm}}{\pi/6}
a = \frac{5 \times 628 \times 10^{-9} \text{ m} \times 6}{\pi}
a \approx 6000 \times 10^{-9} \text{ m}
a = 6 \mu m
The width of the slit is \(6 \mu m\).

According to Einstein's photoelectric equation, the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted electron is:
KE_{\text{max}} = \frac{hc}{\lambda} - \phi
The momentum (p) of the electron is related to its kinetic energy by:
p = \sqrt{2mKE_{\text{max}}}
Substituting the expression for \(KE_{\text{max}}\):
p = \sqrt{2m\left(\frac{hc}{\lambda} - \phi\right)}
When an electron moves in a magnetic field perpendicular to its velocity, it follows a circular path. The distance between points A and B, where the electron starts and hits back the plate, is the diameter of this circular path, \(d_{A-B} = 2R\).
The radius (R) of the circular path is given by:
R = \frac{p}{qB}
Here, the charge \(q\) is \(e\) (charge of an electron). So,
R = \frac{p}{\text{eB}}
Therefore, the distance \(d_{A-B}\) is:
d_{A-B} = 2R = 2\left(\frac{p}{\text{eB}}\right)
Substitute the expression for \(p\):
d_{A-B} = 2\frac{\sqrt{2m\left(\frac{hc}{\lambda} - \phi\right)}}{\text{eB}}
To bring the factor of 2 inside the square root, it becomes \(\sqrt{2^2} = \sqrt{4}\):
d_{A-B} = \frac{\sqrt{4 \cdot 2m\left(\frac{hc}{\lambda} - \phi\right)}}{\text{eB}}
d_{A-B} = \frac{\sqrt{8m\left(\frac{hc}{\lambda} - \phi\right)}}{\text{eB}}

